From 0e1839fac2974d1314111848cded155b054816f5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JSwetz <Johnswetz1982@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 19:42:47 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] feat(tools): align a vendor STEP by ISO 13399 tool coordinate
 systems

Wire the ISO 13399 coordinate system reader into the tool-body pipeline
so vendor tool STEP packages carrying named coordinate systems align
automatically without manual numeric offsets.

- ToolCsysReport::align_tool_body() extracts the tool axis (from CIP
  or CSIF) and calculates the tip offset (-dot(origin, axis)) so that
  the cutting reference point sits at Z = 0 in the tool frame, pointing
  up into the spindle along +Z.
- ToolBodySpec::from_step_auto(&path) reads both mesh facets and coordinate
  systems, aligning with no manual offsets.
- SetToolBody::from_step_auto(&world, tool_name, &path) attaches the auto-aligned
  tool body as one undoable command.
- tool_body_view::load_aligned(&path) returns LoadedBody carrying the auto-alignment
  result or fallback reason, preserving load backwards compatibility.
- In the tool editor (ToolBodyUi), importing a STEP file with ISO 13399 coordinate
  systems automatically sets the axis and tip offset with the note
  'aligned by ISO 13399 coordinate systems'; files without them or with
  degenerate/ambiguous coordinate systems fall back to default numeric placement
  ([0, 0, 1] axis, 0 offset) with a note naming why.
- Test fixture testdata/models/tool_csys_box_iso13399.step combining a B-rep box
  and ISO 13399 placements.
- docs/decisions/370-tool-csys-align.md.

Fork synced at v0.16.0.

Changelog: Tool bodies: align a vendor STEP by its ISO 13399 tool coordinate systems (#370)

Fixes #370
